    @tableofcontents

    Contents of this documentation:
    - @ref sqlite3intro

    @section sqlite3intro Introduction to the sqlite3 Module

    The \c sqlite3 module provides an <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/index.html">SQLite v3</a> driver to %Qore, allowing
    %Qore programs to access SQLite3 database files and/or in-memory databases through the \c %Datasource and
    \c %DatasourcePool classes.

    This module is released under a choice of two licenses:
    - <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a>
    - MIT (see COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for more information)
    .
    The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how
    the %Qore library was initialized).

    Example of creating a SQLite Datasource:
    @code
# user and password are not present as they are ignored
Datasource ds("sqlite3:@/tmp/my-file.sqlite(utf8)");
    @endcode

    The DB name can be a real path to an SQLite3 DB file or the \c ":memory:" string to use an empty in-memory
    database. Please refer to the SQLite3 documentation for more information on how the SQLite library opens
    databases.

    This driver supports the following DBI capabilities:
    - \c DBI_CAP_TRANSACTION_MANAGEMENT
    - \c DBI_CAP_CHARSET_SUPPORT
    - \c DBI_CAP_LOB_SUPPORT
    - \c DBI_CAP_BIND_BY_VALUE
    - \c DBI_CAP_HAS_EXECRAW
    - \c DBI_CAP_HAS_STATEMENT
    - \c DBI_CAP_HAS_NUMBER_SUPPORT

    The driver employs efficient binary bindings for all supported data types when selecting and binding by value.

    The \c Datasource::getServerVersion() method is implemented for this driver and returns a string in
    \c MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH notation - e.g. \c "3.6.7".  \c Datasource::getClientVersion() returns the same information.

    Like all Qore components, the \c sqlite3 driver is thread-safe.

    @subsection sqlite3_binding_placeholders Bind Placeholders

    The sqlite3 driver supports the following bind placeholder formats in SQL strings:

    |!Placeholder|!Description
    |\c %v|Bind by value - the value is bound as a parameter to the prepared statement. This is the safest method as it prevents SQL injection.
    |\c %d|Numeric substitution - the value is converted to a number and substituted directly into the SQL string.
    |\c %s|String substitution - the value is substituted directly into the SQL string. @warning Use with caution as this can lead to SQL injection if used with untrusted input. This is intended for table/column names that cannot be bound as parameters.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# Safe binding with %v
hash<auto> result = ds.select("select * from users where id = %v", user_id);

# Table name substitution with %s (use only with trusted input)
result = ds.select("select * from %s where active = %v", "users", True);

# Numeric substitution with %d
result = ds.select("select * from orders where amount > %d", min_amount);
    @endcode

    @subsection sqlite3_sqlstatement Using SQLStatement

    The sqlite3 driver supports the SQLStatement API for prepared statements:

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# Create a prepared statement
AbstractSQLStatement stmt = ds.getSQLStatement();
on_error stmt.rollback();
on_success stmt.commit();

# Prepare and execute
stmt.prepare("select id, name from users where status = %v", "active");

# Fetch rows one at a time
while (stmt.next()) {
    hash<auto> row = stmt.fetchRow();
    printf("User: %d - %s\n", row.id, row.name);
}

# Or fetch in batches
stmt.prepare("select * from large_table");
while (*list<hash<auto>> rows = stmt.fetchRows(100)) {
    # Process batch of 100 rows
    map process_row($1), rows;
}

# Or fetch a column-oriented batch when built with columnar DBI support
stmt.prepare("select id, name from users where status = %v", "active");
ColumnarResult columns = stmt.fetchColumnar();
    @endcode

    @subsection sqlite3_exceptions Exceptions

    The sqlite3 driver can throw the following exceptions:

    |!Exception|!Description
    |\c DATASOURCE-MISSING-DBNAME|Raised when attempting to open a datasource without a database name
    |\c SQLITE3-CONNECT-ERROR|Raised when the connection to the database file fails
    |\c SQLITE3-BEGIN-ERROR|Raised when starting a transaction fails
    |\c SQLITE3-COMMIT-ERROR|Raised when committing a transaction fails
    |\c SQLITE3-ROLLBACK-ERROR|Raised when rolling back a transaction fails
    |\c SQLITE3-PARSE-EXCEPTION|Raised when parsing bind placeholders fails (invalid format)
    |\c SQLITE3-BIND-EXCEPTION|Raised when binding a parameter fails (unsupported type, etc.)
    |\c SQLITE3-SELECT-ROWS|Raised when executing a select query fails
    |\c SQLITE3-EXEC|Raised when executing a statement fails
    |\c SQLITE3-EXECRAW|Raised when executing a raw statement fails
    |\c SQLITE3-PREPARE-ERROR|Raised when preparing a statement fails
    |\c SQLITE3-FETCH-ROW-ERROR|Raised when fetching a row fails (e.g., next() not called)
    |\c SQLITE3-FETCH-ROWS-ERROR|Raised when fetching rows fails (statement inactive)
    |\c SQLITE3-FETCH-COLUMNS-ERROR|Raised when fetching columns fails (statement inactive)
    |\c SQLITE3-DESCRIBE-ERROR|Raised when describe() is called (not supported)
    |\c SQLITE3-GET-OUTPUT-ERROR|Raised when getOutput() is called (stored procedures not supported)
    |\c SQLITE3-GET-OUTPUT-ROWS-ERROR|Raised when getOutputRows() is called (stored procedures not supported)
    |\c SQLITE3-BIND-PLACEHOLDERS-ERROR|Raised when bindPlaceholders() is called (not supported)
    |\c SQLITE3-STATEMENT-BIND-ERROR|Raised when binding values to a prepared statement fails

    @subsection sqlite3_thread_safety Thread Safety

    The sqlite3 driver is thread-safe. However, when using a single \c Datasource object from multiple threads,
    you should use a \c DatasourcePool to manage concurrent access:

    @code{.py}
# Create a pool with 5-20 connections
DatasourcePool pool("sqlite3:@/path/to/db.sqlite", 5, 20);

# Each thread gets its own connection from the pool
background sub() {
    pool.beginTransaction();
    on_error pool.rollback();
    on_success pool.commit();
    pool.exec("insert into log (msg) values (%v)", "thread message");
}();
    @endcode

    @note When multiple threads write to the same SQLite database, they may encounter \c SQLITE_BUSY errors
    if the database is locked. The driver will raise a \c SQLITE3-* exception in this case.

    @subsection sqlite3_binding_by_value Binding By Value

    SQLite implements different data type handling compared to other DB servers and engines.  Please refer to the
    SQLite documentation in this case as well.

    Data taken from SQLite is converted as follows:
    |!Sqlite3 Column Affinity|!%Qore type|!Description
    |\c INTEGER|\c Type::Int|For integer data
    |\c FLOAT|\c Type::Float|Floating-point data
    |\c TEXT|\c Type::String|All text based data
    |\c BLOB|\c Type::Binary|Raw binary data

    |!QoreType|!SQLite Type|!Description
    |\c int|\c INTEGER|Bound as 64-bit integers
    |\c float|\c FLOAT|Qore float data is converted directly to SQLite float data
    |\c string|\c TEXT|The character encoding is converted to the encoding specified for the connection if necessary
    |\c bool|\c INTEGER|Bound as either \c 0 or \c 1
    |\c date|\c STRING|Date-time values are converted to an ISO-8601 format and stored as strings
    |\c number|\c STRING|Arbitrary-precision numeric data is converted and stored as a string
    |\c binary|\c BLOB|Binary data is stored directly
    |\c NULL / \c NOTHING|\c NULL|Qore NULL and NOTHING values are bound as SQLite NULL

    @section sqlite3releasenotes Release Notes

    @subsection sqlite_1_2_0 sqlite3 Driver Version 1.2.0
    - added support for interruptible I/O for graceful termination of sandboxed programs; interrupt
      checks are performed before database open, statement preparation, and periodically during fetch
      operations
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5116">issue 5116</a>)
    - fixed BLOB binding memory safety issue: now uses \c SQLITE_TRANSIENT to ensure SQLite makes a copy
      of binary data, preventing potential use-after-free bugs
    - fixed integer truncation: now uses \c sqlite3_column_int64() instead of \c sqlite3_column_int()
      to properly handle 64-bit integers
    - fixed SQL parser quote handling: bind placeholders inside quoted strings are now correctly ignored
    - added proper error checking for \c sqlite3_step() return values: now distinguishes between
      \c SQLITE_DONE (success) and error conditions like \c SQLITE_BUSY or \c SQLITE_ERROR
    - added \c DBI_CAP_HAS_STATEMENT capability to properly advertise prepared statement support
    - added GitLab CI configuration with k8s runner support
    - improved documentation with bind placeholder reference and exception documentation
    - added \c SQLStatement::fetchColumnar() support when built against %Qore with columnar DBI APIs

    @subsection sqlite_1_1_0 sqlite3 Driver Version 1.1.0
    - added support for the SQL statement API
    - added support for character encoding handling

    @subsection sqlite_1_0 sqlite3 Driver Version 1.0
    - initial release of the driver
